@@s001 Code: Dijkstra in Java
@@s002 Code: Dijkstra in Java
@@s003 Dijkstra with a PriorityQueue: provisional distances, priority queue with a comparator, relaxation and the stale-entry technique.
@@s004 Weighted graph and distances
@@s005 Each edge is a pair <code>{neighbour, weight}</code> in the list of the node. Distances start from infinity, except for the source.
@@s006 Weighted graph and distances
@@s007 A list of arrays: each neighbour carries the weight of the edge with it.
@@s008 <code>Integer.MAX_VALUE</code> serves as infinity. Be careful when adding to it: it overflows.
@@s009 Syntax notes
@@s010 <code>new int[]{v, w}</code> creates an array already filled with these values: the syntax is an <i>array initializer</i>.
@@s011 Priority queue and relaxation
@@s012 The queue has the smallest distance at the head. Each time I find a better distance, I put it back in the queue.
@@s013 Priority queue and relaxation
@@s014 <code>PriorityQueue</code> is a heap: <code>poll</code> always gives the minimum element according to the comparator.
@@s015 Stale entry: the distance in the queue has already been beaten. I skip the entry: it is the <i>lazy deletion</i> technique.
@@s016 Relaxation: going through <code>u</code> is cheaper than what I already know.
@@s017 Syntax notes
@@s018 The <code>continue</code> jumps to the next round of the <code>while</code>.
@@s019 No <code>decrease-key</code> is needed: several copies are inserted and the old ones are discarded. Cost <code>O(m log m)</code>, equivalent to <code>O(m log n)</code>.
@@s020 Running it
@@s021 Example of input and output (actually compiled and run).
